Coded Variance Noise Modulation

MATLAB simulations for the paper Coded Variance Noise Modulation. The single
MATLAB function run_cvnm_simulations.m generates all six numerical panels in
Figs. 2 and 3. Fig. 1 is a transceiver diagram and is not generated by this
code.

Running the code

The code targets base MATLAB R2025b; no external datasets or toolboxes are
needed. Open the folder containing run_cvnm_simulations.m in MATLAB. To check
the setup before starting the full run:

    testOut = run_cvnm_simulations('smoke');

The smoke test uses 2,000 bits per operating point and reduced parameter
grids. It checks the workflow; do not use its curves to evaluate low error
probabilities. For the full figures, run:

    out = run_cvnm_simulations;

Full mode uses 20,000,000 Monte Carlo bit trials per scheme and operating
point. The separate semi-analytical fading averages in Figs. 3(a,b) use 20,000
channel windows, not 20 million bit trials. The full sweeps are
computationally intensive.

Figures and data

  Panel    What is compared
  -------- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  2(a)     Bit error probability (BEP) versus bit-energy-to-noise ratio in AWGN.
  2(b)     BEP versus bit-energy-to-noise ratio in flat Rayleigh fading.
  2(c)     Zero-threshold CVNM/I-CVNM in AWGN with residual DC offset or static I/Q imbalance, applied separately.
  3(a,b)   Time-selective Rayleigh fading: BEP versus bit-energy-to-noise ratio at fixed correlation (a), and versus correlation at fixed bit-energy-to-noise ratio (b).
  3(c)     Contiguous-to-interleaved BEP ratio versus samples per bit at matched variance contrasts. A ratio above one favors interleaving.

Each run creates a new timestamped directory under cvnm_results/. The function
returns its path. It contains the vector plots (Fig2.pdf, Fig3.pdf), editable
MATLAB figures (.fig), and numerical results in nominal/, frontend/, order/,
and summary/. The latter includes nmse.csv for the two sweeps in Fig. 3(a,b)
and ratio_summary.csv for Fig. 3(c).

To redraw the figures without repeating any simulations, keep the run
directory intact and pass its path back to the function:

    run_cvnm_simulations('redraw',out);

Redraw replaces only the figure files. To check a low-BEP marker, start with
the recorded numbers of errors and decisions: at 20 million bits, one error
corresponds to a BEP estimate of 0.00000005. The script fixes the random seeds
for repeat runs.
